{
  "name": "Maintaining Your Cabin's Finish: A Timeline",
  "step": [
    {
      "name": "Step 1",
      "text": "Keeping your log or cabin finish looking great and protected requires a bit of a routine, but it's totally worth it! Think of it like a schedule for your cabin's skin. Generally, you're looking at re-staining every 4–6 years. However, those south and west-facing walls, which get hit with the most sun and weather, might need a little extra love and attention sooner than the rest. Some homes can even go 5–7 years between full re-stains if they're well-maintained. The good news is, annual cleaning can actually stretch out that re-staining interval! Just a little regular care goes a long way. Beyond the full re-stain, it's a good idea to do a quick visual check annually, especially after harsh weather. Look for any signs of fading, peeling, or if water isn't beading up on the surface. Catching these small issues early can prevent them from becoming bigger, more expensive problems. If you're ever unsure about your cabin's maintenance needs or if you spot something concerning, it's always smart to get a professional opinion. We can help you set up a maintenance plan that works for your cabin and keep it protected for years to come.",
      "@type": "HowToStep",
      "position": 1
    }
  ],
